{
  "abstract": "A model can solve more sampled math continuations when shown one of its own verified solutions, but that conditional advantage may not become a stronger context-free policy. We test this source-to-learner gap in Qwen3-0.6B-Base after a fixed 50-update RLVR warm start. From 1,024 preselected training questions, 636 eligible independent student prefixes receive four native or verified-solution-context continuations. Context raises mean correctness at four samples from 63.60% to 80.97% (+17.37 percentage points, paired 95% interval [14.82, 19.93]). We retain the first boxed-correct EOS suffix and fit three actors for 80 fixed updates: contextual suffixes, native suffixes, and whole verified traces. On a fixed 1,008-question greedy development panel, the contextual actor scores 443/1008, compared with 445 warm, 454 native-control, and 449 whole-trace-control answers. The prespecified conjunction gate requires gains of 3, 2 and 2 points with positive paired lower bounds; observed contextual differences are −0.20, −1.09 and −0.60 points, so the gate fails. The result closes this hard-target bridge dose while leaving dense/on-policy feedback, reset procedures and independent replications open. A compact supplement regrades all extracted records and reproduces the gate.",
